217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 124 0 R
/T (Over the past three decades, European countries have been among the most significant funders of international health initiatives. While the United States has remained the largest single contributor, the EU member states collectively provide comparable levels of financial support. Notably, if anticipated cuts in US global health funding materialize, Europe will become the largest funding bloc in global health, filling critical financial gaps. However, despite their financial contributions, EU institutions have historically struggled to translate their economic power into geopolitical influence in shaping global health governance.)
/E (Over the past three decades, European countries have been among the most significant funders of international health initiatives. While the United States has remained the largest single contributor, the EU member states collectively provide comparable levels of financial support. Notably, if anticipated cuts in US global health funding materialize, Europe will become the largest funding bloc in global health, filling critical financial gaps. However, despite their financial contributions, EU institutions have historically struggled to translate their economic power into geopolitical influence in shaping global health governance.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
